WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:02.333
Many file bugs are timing and state bugs.

2
00:00:02.413 --> 00:00:03.977
The application checks one thing,

3
00:00:04.057 --> 00:00:06.699
then later uses a path or file that may no
longer be the same.

4
00:00:06.779 --> 00:00:08.936
Read and write only within approved
locations,

5
00:00:09.016 --> 00:00:12.952
and avoid read-modify-write logic that can
be confused by concurrent requests unless

6
00:00:13.032 --> 00:00:14.488
the workflow is designed for it.

7
00:00:14.568 --> 00:00:17.372
If multiple users, workers, or jobs can
touch the same record,

8
00:00:17.452 --> 00:00:21.010
the file operation should be coordinated
with the application's state model.

9
00:00:21.090 --> 00:00:23.139
Temporary files deserve deliberate design.

10
00:00:23.219 --> 00:00:26.131
Use safe temporary directories and
generated temporary names.

11
00:00:26.211 --> 00:00:30.039
Java temporary-file APIs or
platform-provided mechanisms are usually better than

12
00:00:30.119 --> 00:00:31.252
predictable filenames.

13
00:00:31.332 --> 00:00:34.729
Temporary locations should be isolated,
access-controlled, monitored,

14
00:00:34.809 --> 00:00:37.828
and cleaned up after success, failure,
timeout, or cancellation.

15
00:00:37.908 --> 00:00:41.575
A temporary file that persists longer than
expected can become a data exposure or

16
00:00:41.655 --> 00:00:43.003
storage exhaustion problem.

17
00:00:43.083 --> 00:00:46.534
Be careful with symlinks, hard links,
rename operations, move operations,

18
00:00:46.614 --> 00:00:49.471
and assumptions that a path still points
to the same file later.

19
00:00:49.551 --> 00:00:53.326
Avoid separate check-then-use patterns
when a file could change between the check

20
00:00:53.406 --> 00:00:54.268
and the operation.

21
00:00:54.348 --> 00:00:57.853
Where appropriate, use atomic write or
replace patterns so readers do not see

22
00:00:57.933 --> 00:00:59.173
partially written output.

23
00:00:59.253 --> 00:01:00.871
Generated reports, cache entries,

24
00:01:00.951 --> 00:01:03.917
and export files should avoid collisions
and incomplete states.

25
00:01:03.997 --> 00:01:06.477
Writable locations should be narrow and
intentional.

26
00:01:06.557 --> 00:01:09.523
Do not write user-controlled data into
executable directories,

27
00:01:09.603 --> 00:01:12.946
source directories, deployment
directories, dependency directories,

28
00:01:13.026 --> 00:01:16.692
classpath locations, or public web roots
unless the design explicitly requires

29
00:01:16.772 --> 00:01:19.252
public static content and has compensating
controls.

30
00:01:19.332 --> 00:01:22.891
The Java process should not need broad
write access simply because one feature

31
00:01:22.971 --> 00:01:23.780
generates a file.
